{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And here am I with you.</b> Onkelos translated: והא מימרי בסעדך — 'and my Memra is your support.' But what is written concerning Moshe (Exodus 3:12), כי אהיה עמך, he translated: ארי יהא מימרי עמך — 'for my Memra shall be with you.' The reason is that the degree of Yaakov is in El Shaddai, not in the distinctive Name; and since he mentioned above the distinctive Name — והנה ה׳ נצב עליו ויאמר אני ה׳ — he needed to translate בסעדך, in the sense of what he said, 'I shall be your help.' But Moshe prophesied in the distinctive Name, so he needed to translate מימרי עמך. And if you object and say: behold, he translated concerning Yaakov (Genesis 46:4), אנכי ארד עמך — אנא איחות עמך — one must answer that the matter compelled him to translate thus, both in the expression איחות — for he did not say אתגלי — and in the expression עמך — for he did not say בסעדך — in order to allude to what is said: they were exiled to Egypt — the Shekhinah was with them."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:49.065440041Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}